[38;2;124;124;124m$(a/1)^n = 0$ for some $n \geq 1$. This means there exists $s \in R \setminus [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathfrak{p}$ with $sa^n = 0$. Since $a$ is a non-zero divisor, $a^n$ is also a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnon-zero divisor, so $sa^n = 0$ implies $s = 0$. But $s \notin \mathfrak{p}$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhile $0 \in \mathfrak{p}$, contradiction. Therefore $a \notin \mathfrak{p}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 2: Dimension inequality.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider any chain of prime ideals in $R/(a)$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\bar{\mathfrak{p}}_0 \subsetneq \bar{\mathfrak{p}}_1 \subsetneq \cdots [0m
